== 2. coverage 示例与 workflow 两句 ==
45:  "coverage": {
97:After implementation is complete and the main session's CASE review is satisfied, continue `case.designSession` with `role: checker` and `purpose: execution`. Before acting, the Checker reads `case.md` through its task-directory pointer line and the two template copies through their `.workloom/templates/` pointer lines. The executor first completes every SR item, then runs C1 through Cn in order and records actual actions, observations, and evidence in `case-result.md`. It collects the task diff's changed executable lines, their intersection with the native coverage report, and the uncovered changed lines under `coverage/` (changed-lines.json, the native report, and diff-coverage.json); large evidence goes under `case-evidence/`. Incremental coverage must be strictly greater than the configured threshold (default 90%, `coverage.threshold`). When it is at or under 90%, the Checker attaches per-file reasons, the developer attaches an explanation, and the main session reviews: unrelated code goes back to the developer for removal, missing case coverage goes back to the Checker for additional cases, and any code or case change requires a full rerun of static review, every C item, and coverage. If the review confirms the shortfall, the main session approves a waiver; otherwise the task is sent back for rework. A blocked C item stops all later C items and prevents a pass. Any code fix requires a full rerun of static review, every C item, and coverage.
113:Core CASE failures — a missing CASE document, a missing design session, an invalid RESULT, failed or pending items, failed standard verification, failed coverage, and an invalid session chain — cannot be bypassed with force. Run `workloom_task_check` only after RESULT has `status: passed`, every SR/C item is passed, standard verification is green, coverage is generated above the configured threshold (default 90%) or is `below_threshold` with per-file reasons plus a valid approved waiver bound to the current code fingerprint (or validly `not_applicable` with a standalone reason line), and Open issues is `- none`.
== 3. packages-scan SKILL：coverage / 旧 test.js 缺席 / 新 .ts 命中 ==
3:description: Scan a project's package structure and produce a candidate packages list with worktree setup commands for `.workloom/config.json`. Detects pnpm/npm workspaces, lerna, nx, turbo, go.work, Cargo workspaces, git submodules, and nested git repos. Also surfaces the source-language stack and proposes `coverage.languages` defaults through the config-edit skill.
15:3. **Propose coverage defaults** — for each detected language that has a built-in default in `LANGUAGE_DEFAULTS` (exported by `packages/core/dist/task/coverage-compute.js`), propose a `coverage.languages` entry with its default `format` and `command` skeleton. Route this proposal through the `workloom-config-edit` skill together with the `packages` candidates. Flag detected languages without a built-in default as requiring a manual `format` and `command`; never guess one.
16:4. **Present the candidates** — show each entry's path, type (`workspace` / `submodule` / `nested-git`), `git` flag, `languages`, and proposed `setup`, together with the proposed `coverage.languages` entries. Briefly state the evidence for each non-empty recipe and flag any uncertain choice. The user may drop or rename entries and edit the commands.
17:5. **User confirms** — apply the requested package, setup, and coverage edits. Resolve uncertain recipes during this confirmation instead of writing guessed commands.
18:6. **Write `.workloom/config.json`** — follow the `workloom-config-edit` skill's modification discipline: read the current file and merged config, show the diff, get confirmation, write atomically, and validate with `workloom-doctor`. Merge the confirmed candidates into the `packages` field and the confirmed proposals into `coverage.languages`:
22:   - existing `coverage.languages` entries are preserved; only newly confirmed languages are added.
59:## Detection coverage
66:- Edits to config fields outside this scan's `packages` and `coverage.languages` proposals go through the `workloom-config-edit` skill.
